WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.240
I want to quickly show you the Django Admin and then touch on test briefly for how you might

00:07.240 --> 00:13.240
run them in Django and then we'll move on to the final project practice.

00:13.240 --> 00:18.800
The Django Admin is really one of the star features of this web framework and it's one

00:18.800 --> 00:21.480
of the reasons why it's so popular.

00:21.480 --> 00:26.520
It lets you easily interact with and edit our database models in a really nice UI with

00:26.520 --> 00:29.600
very, very minimal setup.

00:29.600 --> 00:34.080
So to use the Admin, you're going to need to create a super user.

00:34.080 --> 00:39.440
So we do that by running Python managepy, create super user.

00:39.440 --> 00:52.720
Don't forget the password that you set.

00:52.720 --> 00:56.240
That will let you know a super user was created successfully.

00:56.240 --> 01:01.280
If we go back to URLs that UI for practical blog, we'll see that there is an admin path

01:01.280 --> 01:02.520
to find here.

01:02.520 --> 01:08.640
You don't have to use the Admin if you don't want to, you would just leave this out.

01:08.640 --> 01:15.360
Now that I've created a super user, I can go to my blog and instead of looking at a post,

01:15.360 --> 01:20.960
I'm going to type in the URL slash admin and I will take me to a login page and I can

01:20.960 --> 01:28.360
now log in with the super user that I created and it's going to take me to this interface.

01:28.360 --> 01:34.940
Now these were created by Django automatically, but here we'll see in the blog apps blog

01:34.940 --> 01:52.940
app are posts and I can click in here and add a post and give it a title and give it a

01:52.940 --> 01:59.740
slog and just save it and we'll see that it was added successfully.

01:59.740 --> 02:05.740
So when we go back to the index for our blog, there it is, that's the second blog post.

02:05.740 --> 02:13.100
And all that I had to do to set that up was create and fill in an admin.py file because

02:13.100 --> 02:19.980
it was already created for us, import our post model and then register it.

02:19.980 --> 02:27.140
So admin.site.registerpost gave us all of that almost for free.

02:27.140 --> 02:33.100
If we wanted to have Django auto-generate our slugs for us when creating a new blog post,

02:33.100 --> 02:38.380
which I think we do because creating any slug manually is pretty annoying, we can comment

02:38.380 --> 02:50.020
this out and instead create a class called post admin that inherits from model admin

02:50.020 --> 02:56.820
and then register this instead.

02:56.820 --> 03:05.540
If I save that and then refresh this page and go to our admin, and we'll notice that Django

03:05.540 --> 03:13.860
is currently running in debug mode, that means that it's going to pick up any changes

03:13.860 --> 03:20.700
automatically and just refresh the server, you don't want that on in production.

03:20.700 --> 03:25.740
So just in FYI, that's a setting that you will flip and settings.py.

03:25.740 --> 03:31.020
If you go to a URL here that doesn't exist, you're going to get a page not founder.

03:31.020 --> 03:35.060
It's going to tell you what it was raised by and then you'll see a message at the bottom

03:35.060 --> 03:39.460
saying you're seeing this error because you have debugged equals true in production,

03:39.460 --> 03:44.100
you probably want to redirect to a standard 404 page.

03:44.100 --> 03:51.060
So now if we look at our admin for post, we will see that when we make a post.

03:51.060 --> 04:00.300
As I'm typing in a title, we'll see that the slug is generated automatically.

04:00.300 --> 04:06.660
The cool thing about this is it skips certain words like A and that you don't really care

04:06.660 --> 04:15.580
about for SEO.

04:15.620 --> 04:23.660
So that is what you get with Django for free.

04:23.660 --> 04:25.820
There's a lot more that you can do with it.

04:25.820 --> 04:29.300
It's very powerful, but I just wanted to give you a preview.

